            <ArticleTitle>The SAR development of substituted purine derivatives as selective CB2 agonists for the treatment of chronic pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>5572-5575</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0960-894X(14)01177-9</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.bmcl.2014.11.006</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Osteoarthritis (OA) and the associated joint pain are highly prevalent and a leading cause of disability. We have previously reported the identification of a series of purines as selective CB2 agonists and the identification of compound 1 as a clinical candidate for the treatment of joint pain. In this article we describe the further SAR development of the purine scaffold leading to the discovery of compound 6 as a potent, CNS penetrating CB2 agonist with high selectivity for CB2 over CB1 and oral efficacy in animal models of chronic OA pain.</AbstractText>
